%S A400842 3,19,31,113,127,131,157,179,223,227,229,251,257,281,293,331,349,421,
%T A400842 467,479,563,571,593,641,647,673,823,859,907,911,1013,1039,1091,1103,
%U A400842 1171,1217,1249,1319,1439,1471,1511,1531,1549,1571,1663,1697,1721,1733,1777,1933,2003,2063,2083,2153,2161,2267,2287,2459,2539,2543,2579,2617,2647,2699,2837,2903,2971,2999,3001,3137,3181,3391
%N A400842 Primes p for which there exists an odd prime q <= p such that 2*((p#/p) / (q#/q)) == -1 mod p, where p# = A034386(p).
%H A400842 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A400842/b400842.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..4005</a>
%H A400842 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%F A400842 a(n) = A000040(1+A400839(n)).
%e A400842 3 is a term because 1 + 2*((3#/3) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 3 when we set also q to 3.
%e A400842 17 is NOT a term: Although 1 + 2*((17#/17) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 17 when we set q to 2, as then (1+(2*A002110(A000720(17)-1)/A002110(A000720(2)-1))) = 60061 = 17*3533; but nevertheless, there is no ODD prime q which would satisfy the same condition. For the same reason, 241 is not included in this sequence.
%e A400842 19 is a term because 1 + 2*((19#/19) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 19 when we set q to 3, in which case the formula reduces to 1 + 17# = 1+A002110(7), which is a multiple of 19, as 510511 = 19*97*277.
%e A400842 113 is a term because 1 + 2*((113#/113) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 113 when we set q to 67, as then 1 + 2*((113#/113) / (67#/67)) = 1+(2*A002110(29)/A002110(18)) = 4770037638436880574299 = 113 * 6607 * 6389090731654789.
%o A400842 (PARI) A400842(n) = prime(1+A400839(n));
%Y A400842 Subsequence of A400843.
%Y A400842 Cf. A000040, A000720, A002110, A034386, A400839, A401052 (gives the corresponding odd prime q, the largest of them, if several exist).
%Y A400842 Cf. also A401060, A401061, A401063 for similar sequences.
%K A400842 nonn,changed
%O A400842 1,1
%A A400842 _Antti Karttunen_, Oct 15 2026
